description:
Symptom: ToolDefinition.schema requires a representative Value, e.g. list([]) for 'list of integers' — but list([]) is empty so the element kind is unspecified. Type-based registry queries (registry-search --output_kind=integer) can't filter by 'list whose elements are integers'.

Fix options:
  (a) extend schema to accept either a sample Value OR a kind annotation, e.g. tagged 'schema/list-of' wrapping the element type;
  (b) require a non-empty sample (one element of each kind a heterogeneous list might contain) — simpler but ergonomically worse for list<integer>;
  (c) adopt a small schema-DSL value form (recursive 'kind: schema, kind: list-of, element: <kind>').

Prefer (a). registry-search's recurseHasKind already does sub-tree traversal; this gives it a canonical hook to recognise.

Symptom: mod-inv returns 'tagged "mod-inv/no-inverse"' on no-inverse cases (foreign-pass-through escape hatch repurposed as in-band error channel). cas-verify returns 'record { equal: false, reason: ... }'. Two tools, two patterns. Downstream consumers must dispatch differently.

Decision needed (ADR): pick one of:
  (a) tagged "<tool>/<error-class>" for fatal/structural failures + record { ok: bool, ... } for routine yes/no decisions (and codify the distinction);
  (b) always record { ok: bool, value?: ..., reason?: ... };
  (c) discriminated union via a 'tagged' kind always, no records-with-flags.

Prefer (a) because it preserves cas-verify's existing convention and gives a clear semantic distinction. Migrate mod-inv if needed for consistency. Add to PRD §6.1.

record{n_bytes} → record{bytes} (hex-encoded). The canonical nondeterministic primitive in the workbench (per ADR 0005). Reads OS entropy via crypto.getRandomValues / /dev/urandom equivalent.

Annotated `nondeterministic: true` in its manifest. Provenance records the run but does not promise re-derivation; downstream consumers re-enter determinism by taking these bytes as a typed input.

Schema:
- input: record{ n_bytes: integer }
- output: record{ bytes: string (hex), source_kind: "os-urandom" | other }

This is the only tool in the workbench that may legitimately fail the determinism contract. It exists to anchor that boundary cleanly.

packages/cas-core/src/verify.ts:15-53 has two near-identical try/catch blocks (lines 16-29 for lhs, lines 30-43 for rhs) constructing an out-of-scope record. Extract a helper:

  function tryToRatFn(v: Value): {ok: true; rf: RatFn} | {ok: false; message: string}

Then casVerify becomes a flat sequence: tryToRatFn(lhs), tryToRatFn(rhs), branch on outcomes. About 25% shorter and the side='\''lhs'\''/'\''rhs'\'' distinction lives at one site.
